---
id: problem
title: "One folder, two repositories"
description: "Sometimes a folder in a monorepo has to be a repository of its own as well: a public project in a private monorepo, a vendored library, a part with its own CI."
series: why-splicing
seriesOrder: 1
topics: [git]
---

I like monorepos. All my code is in one place, one commit can change several projects at once, and I test them together. But again and again, one folder of my monorepo also has to be a repository of its own. Here are three situations where that happens to me.

## A public folder in a private monorepo

My monorepo is private. It has my website, my CV and a few tools. One of the tools I want to publish as open source. Not the whole monorepo, only that one folder.

Publishing it once isn't enough. People open issues and pull requests, a bot updates the dependencies. Those commits have to come back into the monorepo, where I keep working on the tool next to everything else.

## Vendoring a library

The other way round: my app uses a library that someone else maintains. I find a bug in it and I can't wait for the next release. So I copy the library's source into my monorepo, fix the bug there and test the fix together with my app.

Then the fix should go upstream. I don't own the library's repository, so the fix first has to reach a branch in my fork, as a library commit without any of my app's code. From there I can open a pull request.

## Separate CI pipelines

The third one is less obvious. GitHub Actions, for example, only runs the workflows in `.github/workflows` at the root of a repository. A workflow in a subfolder never runs.

But the library deserves its own pipeline. It tests the library on its own, for contributors who never see my monorepo. My monorepo's pipeline tests everything together. I want both, and for the first one the folder has to be a repository.

## The same problem three times

In all three cases I want to work in the monorepo as if the folder were an ordinary folder. At the same time, the folder should exist as a repository of its own, and commits should go both ways.

Git has two answers for this: submodules and subtrees. In the next episodes I show how each of them works, and what goes wrong.

**Have you ever had to decide between a monorepo and separate repos?** Write it in the comments below.
